>> title: Oyakodon
>> category: Rice, Beans & Grains
>> tags: japanese, chicken, egg, rice bowl, donburi, weeknight
>> counters: Japanese Home Cooking
>> aka: 親子丼, oyakodon, oyakodon, おやこどん, 親子丼ぶり, oyako donburi, chicken and egg rice bowl, chicken and egg donburi
>> pairs-with: dashi, sumashi-jiru, sunomono
>> servings: 2
>> time: 20 min
>> slack: unforgiving — the second pour is the dish and its window is about thirty seconds wide; once it sets you have an omelette sitting on rice, which is a different and worse thing, and there is no unsetting it
>> step.1: The egg goes in twice. The first pour cooks; the second barely sets and is what makes it 親子丼 rather than chicken and scrambled egg. 割り下: dashi 4 : mirin 1 : soy 1.
>> step.2: simmer 3 min
>> step.3: simmer the chicken 5 min
>> step.4: pour two-thirds, set 60 sec
>> step.5: pour the rest, lid off the heat

The egg goes in twice. The first pour cooks through; the second barely sets, and that is what makes this 親子丼 rather than chicken with scrambled egg on it. The liquid is 割り下 — dashi 4 : mirin 1 : soy 1.

Simmer @onion{1}(sliced 1/4 inch, with the grain so it holds) in @dashi{3/4%cup}(180 mL), @mirin{3%Tbs}(45 mL) and @soy sauce{3%Tbs}(45 mL) in a #small frying pan{} ~simmer{3%min}, until the edges have gone soft.

Lay @chicken thighs{7%oz}(200 g; boneless, in 1-inch pieces) into @&(~1)onion{} in one layer and ~simmer{5%min}, turning them once, until they are just cooked through.

Pour two-thirds of @eggs{3}(beaten four or five strokes only, so there are still ribbons of white) over @&(~1)chicken{} in a spiral and ~set{60%sec} without stirring or shaking it.

Pour the last of the egg over @&(~1)pan{} with @mitsuba{4%sprigs}(in 1-inch lengths), put the lid on, take it off the heat to ~stand{30%sec}, and slide the whole thing onto @cooked rice{2%bowls}(hot) while the top is still glossy.
